  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run succeeded with no alerts firing.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (time field `timestamp`) is web server access logs. Each record has:\n- the request (`url`, `request`, `extension`), the HTTP response code (`response`, stored as text) and `bytes` sent;\n- the visitor (`clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `referer`);\n- location (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`);\n- `tags`, `memory`, and an `hour_of_day` field computed from the timestamp.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-14508e89 Web logs overview\"** (id `cb441109-ac28-4f3f-a9f4-7378cdcf46f2`, default time range last 7 days). It has 8 panels, all built on the sample data view:\n- Single numbers for total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average bytes per request and total bytes served\n- A line chart of requests over time\n- A donut chart of requests by response code\n- A table of the top 10 requested URLs\n- A table of the top 10 source countries, with unique visitors per country\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-14508e89 5xx spike\"** (id `c9616701-b578-48bc-a50e-f7822cc7d60d`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts records matching `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword < \"600\"` over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThree things to know about the rule:\n- **How the 5xx filter works:** the response code is stored as text, not a number, so the filter compares strings. That's only correct because all the codes are three digits.\n- **It doesn't skip earlier hits:** each run counts the whole 5-minute window, including requests an earlier run already saw. Otherwise each run would count only the newest requests and undercount the window.\n- **It only creates alerts:** no notification (email, Slack, etc.) is attached, because none was asked for. Alerts will show up in Kibana's alerts view.\n\nI didn't check against the data that the filter actually matches 5xx records.\n\nThe scripts I used to build these are in the scratchpad (`dash.py`, `rule.json`).",
